===Australia vs Japan===
{{see also|Australia–Japan football rivalry}}
This fixture was Australia's first game in the World Cup finals since 1974.<ref >{{Cite web |last=Maasdorp |first=James |date=12 Jun 2016 |title=Looking back: Socceroos beat Japan in 2006 World Cup, 10 years on |website=ABC News |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-06-12/socceroos-beat-japan-in-world-cup-10-years-on/7499910}}</ref> Prior to the game, Japan officials had reportedly suggested that Australia were an aggressive team prone to "dirty tackles".<ref name="TheGuardian_AusJapan">{{Cite web |website=The Guardian| last=van Happen |first=Jan|date=13 Jun 2006 |title=Cahill's call to action inspires Socceroos' dramatic finale |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jun/13/worldcup2006.match1}}</ref>

Australia had the best of the chances early in the first half, with Japan goalkeeper [[Yoshikatsu Kawaguchi]] saving twice from Australia striker [[Mark Viduka]] in the opening five minutes. In the 25th minute, Kawaguchi performed a fine save from a powerful strike taken by [[Mark Bresciano]].<ref name="TheGuardian_AusJapan"/>

Shortly after, Japan took the lead in controversial circumstances after 26 minutes when Socceroos keeper [[Mark Schwarzer]] was impeded as he was trying to collect [[Shunsuke Nakamura|Shunsuke Nakamura's]] cross.<ref name="BBC_AUS_JPN_HIDDINK_DELIGHT">{{Cite web |date=12 June 2006 |website=BBC Sport - World Cup 2006 |title='Deserved' win delights Hiddink |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/australia/5043868.stm}}</ref>

Late in the second half, with Australia pressing forward for an equalizer, Kawaguchi failed to claim a long [[throw in]] from Lucas Neill. Substitute [[Tim Cahill]], who was reportedly recovering from knee ligament damage that threatened to rule him out of the tournament,<ref name="TheGuardian_AusJapan"/> struck the loose ball to score Australia's first World Cup finals goal.<ref name="BBC_AUS_JAPAN">{{Cite web |date=12 June 2006 |website=BBC Sport - World Cup 2006 |title=Australia 3-1 Japan |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4852706.stm}}</ref>

Minutes later, Cahill found space on the edge of the penalty box and struck from long range, with the ball hitting the left post and bouncing into the goal on the right side netting, putting Australia ahead.<ref name="BBC_AUS_JAPAN"/> Deep into injury time, substitute [[John Aloisi]] ran through the Japan defence and scored Australia's third goal via a low left-footed strike,<ref name="BBC_AUS_JAPAN"/> sealing Australia's first ever win at the World Cup.

After the game, Australia coach [[Guus Hiddink]] expressed delight with the resilience shown by his players, however was adamant that Japan's opening goal should not have stood.<ref name="BBC_AUS_JPN_HIDDINK_DELIGHT"/> Schwarzer revealed that the Egyptian referee [[Essam Abdel-Fatah]] had apologised for allowing Japan's opening goal to stand instead of granting a free-kick, and expressed relief that his decision did not affect the final outcome of the game.<ref name="TheGuardian_AusJapan"/> Japan coach [[Zico (footballer)|Zico]] remarked that referees should not have to make such apologies and that FIFA need to sort out such incidents.<ref >{{Cite web |website=ABC News |date=16 Jun 2006 |title=Zico angry over Cahill penalty comments |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2006-06-16/zico-angry-over-cahill-penalty-comments/1779406}}</ref>

This was Japan's third straight failure at winning their opening fixture at the World Cup finals, having lost in [[1998 FIFA World Cup Group H#Argentina vs Japan|1998 to Argentina 1–0]] and drawing in [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group H#Japan vs Belgium|2002 to Belgium 2–2]].<ref name="BBC_AUS_JAPAN"/>

