===Japan vs Brazil===
Brazil came into the fixture having already qualified for the round of 16, with opening wins against Croatia and Australia. Brazil had come under criticism for lack of flair in their performances, and their star player [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]], who was the top scorer at the [[2002 FIFA World Cup|previous World Cup]], had yet to score and was close to being benched.<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil">{{Cite web |website=BBC Sport |date=22 June 2006 |title=Japan 1-4 Brazil |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853416.stm}}</ref> This fixture was Ronaldo's 17th successive World Cup match, the longest streak by a current player at that time.<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil"/>

Japan took a surprise early lead after 34 minutes against the highly-fancied Brazilians when [[Keiji_Tamada|Keiji Tamada's]] left-foot strike beat [[Dida (footballer, born 1973)|Dida]] on his near post. Just before half-time, Brazil striker [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]] equalized via a close-range header.<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil"/>

After the break, Brazil doubled their advantage via a long-range strike taken by [[Juninho Pernambucano]]. [[Gilberto (footballer, born_1976)|Gilberto]] later scored via an acute angle, and Ronaldo again scored via a long-range strike into the bottom corner to secure a 4–1 victory for Brazil.<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil"/>

With the victory, Brazil completed 10 successive victories at the World Cup.<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil"/> Japan's goal was the first time Brazil had conceded in 461 minutes of football.<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil"/> Ronaldo's 13th and 14th World Cup goals equalled the all-time record of 14 set by West Germany's [[Gerd Müller]],<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil"/> and he also became the 20th player to score at three different World Cups.<ref name="BBCJapanBrazil"/>

