===Croatia vs Australia===
The fixture was marred by numerous [[List of 2006 FIFA World Cup controversies#Croatia vs. Australia (group stage)|referring mistakes and controversies]], resulting in referee [[Graham Poll]] being dismissed from the remainder of the tournament.<ref >{{Cite web |website=BBC Sport |date=28 June 2006 |title=Ref Poll sent home from World Cup |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/5108722.stm}}</ref> Shortly after, Poll announced his retirement from international football altogether.<ref >{{Cite web |website=The Guardian|last=Lutz |first=Tom |date=30 Jun 2006 |title=Poll retires from international game |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jun/29/worldcup2006.sport26}}</ref>

In an interview in 2022, former Australia forward [[John Aloisi]] recalled the fixture being 'pure chaos'.<ref >{{Cite web |last=Bungard |website=Nine.com.au| first=Matt |date=17 November 2022 |title=EXCLUSIVE: John Aloisi recalls 'pure chaos' that rocked World Cup in Socceroos' clash with Croatia |url=https://www.nine.com.au/sport/football/fifa-world-cup-looking-back-at-socceroos-draw-with-croatia-in-2006-20221117-p5j2fm.html}}</ref>

With the draw against Croatia, Australia progressed from the group stages of the World Cup for the first time in their history, and coach [[Guus Hiddink]] for the third time in his career, after Netherlands in [[1998 FIFA World Cup|1998]] and South Korea in [[2002 FIFA World Cup|2002]].<ref >{{Cite web |website=BBC Sport|date=22 June 2006 |title=Croatia 2-2 Australia |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853428.stm}}</ref>
